<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michael the Brave</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1558">1558</a>–<a href="/years/1601">1601</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Mihai Viteazul</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michael the Brave (Mihai Viteazul) was prince of Wallachia from 1593 to 1601. He rebelled against Ottoman overlordship, winning at Călugăreni in 1595, and in 1599 and 1600 took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> and Moldavia, briefly uniting the three principalities under one ruler. His rule soon collapsed and he was assassinated in 1601, but he later became a symbol of Romanian unity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michael-the-brave.jpg" alt="Michael the Brave" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aegidius Sadeler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MihaiViteazul.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ottoman control tightened after 1538, and the chronic contest among boyar factions and claimants, each seeking support in Istanbul, Poland or Transylvania, wore down the principality’s autonomy; <b>Michael the Brave of Wallachia</b> held it briefly in 1600.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Society rested on the boyars, the landowning class whose coalitions made and unmade princes, and on a peasantry whose lot worsened under Ottoman economic hegemony: serfdom was established under <b>Michael the Brave</b> to increase manorial revenues and effectively abolished by Constantine Mavrocordatos in 1746.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
